 suppose but  structuring that is is where the work  comes in the process for writing is very  different depending on what you're  writing for so I'm lucky that I get to  do stand-up and I get to write for  television and the two disciplines are  wildly different for me anyway  stand-up is a lot more straightforward  because you can go in try ideas out and  you can find out conclusively if  something has humor in it or is going to  make a group of people laugh by just  taking a germ of an idea and going to a  small comedy club and just saying it and  that appeals very much to my personality  is instant gratification it very much  appeals to me as my family would tell  you it's just like can I have it now can  we just do it now and with stand-up you  go out you're half an idea you're going  to say on a stage and if someone laughs  you think yeah okay there's something in  that writing for television is entirely  different because there's no audience  there during the whole process so you're  just hoping that you're right that this  is funny  and that it's gonna chime with people  I've just worked out certain key things  that work for me  when writing and that is one not to be  on my own and so I always get other  people in who I find funny to throw  ideas against it's sort of creating that  atmosphere really of saying a thing out  loud and seeing if it's funny and then  it's log writing for TV it's just  graft one of the few times you'll see me  irritated is if I hear someone say that  man Dame's just slapped is just a bit of  slapstick in furia it's the one thing  that drives me crazy because it isn't  just a bit of slapstick it is painfully  planned you know and everything is you  know if you saw my on the walls of my  flat when we're writing man Dana is a  wash with post-it notes having a germ of  a funny idea is very different from  making it sit within within a coherent  structure so that for me is the battle  is is making something fit together and  the only way to do that is just to keep  bashing your head against the wall it's  just to keep hitting it until it all  sits in place does that make sense but I  think how closely you stick to a script  and how much you improvise is entirely  dependent on the project because if it's  mundane then I feel entirely comfortable  at coming off the page and trying new  things out and and altering it as we go  along but if it's someone else's like  I'm in other shows I mean the show  called cuckoo I think you have to be  respectful of the of the process because  I know how much works going to my show I  don't Bowl into other people's shows you  fear my if I'm lucky enough to be on  them and start and start saying I'm  going to change this but an ideal set is  a group of people who are so comfortable  with each other that everyone's got a  voice and people can say how about this  how about we try this and in mand I'm  often you know things will come from  someone suggesting it on the set and I  love it I don't know I don't know how to  keep things spontaneous and fresh when  you're saying and repeatedly I don't  know I think you just do it but it is  often tedious that's the truth of it  that filming is often tedious often when  you do the first take of a scene for  television the crew will laugh and you  know if you when you do the rehearsal  for the crew they'll laugh and if you're  a performer like may you feed off that  and with every take that diminishes yeah  it's one of the things you just have to  learn is is to try and be consistent  it's just a slog it's just a learnt  scale I suppose and it is it's difficult  because the words start to become  meaningless it's likely anything if you  repeat any words over and over again  after a while it just sounds like you're  speaking in tongues and that's often the  case with filming yeah so it's just yeah  it's just a thing to do it the secret of  successful collaboration is just  remembering that it's everybody's that  how much everybody is invested in it I  think that's the thing to remember that  even if someone's playing a small part  in something it is significant to them  and it is part of a bigger dream of  theirs just make everyone feel important  I suppose because they are now on a show  like man down it's such an ensemble  piece it's such it's so reliant on the  interaction between the characters and  everybody feeling like they're being  funny and having fun it's one of the  first things I learned in stand-up that  Logan who run that course said you know  if you're having a good time chances are  the audience will I think it's the best  bit of advice I ever got and and if you  remember that on set if everybody is  having a good time and feeling valued  and comfortable and the chances are that  that what they what you end up watching  will be enjoyable I suppose what I enjoy  about my job is that I would be doing  this even if I wasn't being paid for it  I was a tedious show off prior to comedy  anyway so you know I do I was a drama  teacher and the kids will tell you the  kids I taught that I was an insufferable  show-off then and my friends would tell  you in pubs for many years I was always  trying to make people laugh  I can't remember Oscar Wilde's exact  quote about doing the thing that you you  know being when you find the thing that  you love to do and then someone starts  paying you for it is the dream I'm sorry  Oscar I can't remember what you said it  doesn't feel like work a lot of the time  I mean don't get me wrong often it does  the writing process feels like work but  a lot of the time it's just such a fun  and it's you know it's so great to make  people laugh or who wouldn't like you  know but if people come and watch one of  my shows and feel like they've you know  taking the day off from there from  whatever's going on in their life and  had some fun escaping it you know or  privilege  [Music]  [Music]

